The '''Conservative Interlude period (1968–1976)''' covers the conservative government's privatisation of the state-run hospitality industry, the return of the Labor Party in coalition with the socialists, and the economic downturn of the 1970s that produced flip-flops between the two parties. The period ended in 1976 with the onset of the [[Camboriú War]] and the oil-shock recession that followed.

== The 1967 hospitality workers walkout ==

In December 1967, all hospitality workers walked out in a two-month strike during the peak travel season before the 1968 election. The strike was a negotiation for higher wages. Hospitality workers' wages were low — the PLG government had not enforced labor protections for the hospitality sector. The strike caused economic disruption during the peak season and was one of the reasons the PPG won the 1968 election. The walkout demonstrated the PLG's lack of care for labor protections in the hospitality industry.

== The conservative government (1968–1972) ==

=== Privatisation of hospitality ===

The conservative government broke up the state-run hospitality industry ([[Programa de Hospitalidad]]) into multiple private companies: three hotel chains, the national airline, and the cruiseline. The privatisation raised funds for manufacturing investment. The privatisation was a core conservative policy, reversing the PLG's state-run hospitality model.

=== Tax policy ===

The conservative government did not change taxes for the first four years (1968–1972). Taxes during this period were pretty low, inherited from the PLG era. The privatisation raised funds, reducing the need for tax increases initially.

=== Manufacturing investment ===

Funds from privatisation were invested in manufacturing. The manufacturing conundrum continued: what could Guajicutea make competitively? The government focused on sectors where local resources or tourism market proximity provided an advantage: food processing (canned fish, cassava flour, tobacco, spirits), construction materials (cement, bricks, tiles from local limestone), textiles and crafts (connected to [[Bikuismo]]), and light assembly of imported components. Guano, formerly a major export from [[Warikú]], was essentially depleted by this era, increasing the pressure to develop tourism and manufacturing as replacements.

== The 1972 election and the PLG-KdG coalition ==

In 1972, the PLG returned in a coalition with the [[Kadarist Party of Guajicutea]] (KdG, socialists). The coalition attempted to renationalise the airline and cruiseline, reversing the conservative privatisation.

== The 1973 inquisition ==

The PLG-KdG coalition launched an inquisition (investigation) into the privatisation of the hospitality industry. The minister of commerce, [[Hernán Montes]], was arrested over the sale. The inquisition was a political persecution of the previous conservative government's officials.

== The 1970s: Camboriú War and oil crises ==

The [[Camboriú War]] and oil crises of the 1970s produced an economic downturn. The downturn caused flip-flops between the PPG and PLG through the decade.

== 1974 tax increase and universal healthcare ==

In 1974, the government raised income tax again to support government coffers and increased spending. The '''Universal Healthcare Act''' was passed in the same year, establishing universal healthcare for all residents. The tax increase and the healthcare act were part of the government's expanding social programmes during this period, though the income tax raise also reflected the drying up of reparations funds.

== Public mood ==

Post-war optimism persisted through this period but was tempered by growing hesitancy in government trust. The PLG's pro-natalist and pro-immigration policies had produced rapid population growth, strain on resources, and tax increases. People had less trust in the government as the consequences of growth-at-all-costs policies became visible. The birth rate was slowing from the peak of the baby boom as the population stabilised and the initial surge was absorbed. Unemployment was tough but recovering, as the new manufacturing experiments funded by privatisation began to produce jobs.

== Political dynamics ==

The [[Partido Laborista de Guajicutea|PLG]] held its assembly majority through the 1960s, but its dominance was slowly chipped away by two forces. The [[Kadarist Party of Guajicutea]] (KdG) — a new socialist party drawing on an Adonian political ideology — gained ground from the left. The [[Partido del Progreso de Guaji|PPG]] gained ground on an anti-immigration platform and a focus on the improvement of daily life. Group wages grew but never rose significantly, as the economy remained services-reliant and import-dependent. Unemployment rose slowly as construction slowed, prompting the manufacturing push.

The political drift produced a divided government by the late 1960s: the PLG retained assembly plurality but lost the presidency. In the 1968 election, the PPG won the presidency, ending two decades of Labor Party governance. The onset of the [[Camboriú War]] in 1976 further destabilised the economy and set the stage for the turbulent decade that followed.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 680,000 in 1960 to approximately 1,400,000 by 1970, driven by the baby boom, pro-natalist policies, and immigration.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Year !! Population !! Growth rate !! Notes
|-
| 1960 || ~680,000 || ~3.3%/yr || Immigration begins. Tourism boom accelerating.
|-
| 1970 || ~1,400,000 || ~6.6%/yr || Peak immigration + natural increase. Fertility 4.5. Density ~427/km².
|}

